gm-method: when a consumer repo must match a sibling SDK '100% the same as source', the published package can silently lag source for a LONG time if its publish CI is broken. Witness the gap by byte-diffing the INSTALLED package's key generated files (e.g. a transpiled artifact) against the sibling source -- a size/line-count mismatch (ignoring CRLF/BOM on win32) is the tell. Then check the CI: `gh run list --workflow <publish> --limit N` for repeated `failure`, and `gh run view <id> --log-failed`. Classic frozen-publish bug: `npm version patch --no-git-tag-version` computes the next version from the repo's package.json (which the commit-back step never advanced because it ran AFTER `npm publish`, so a publish failure aborted before it) -> every run recomputes the SAME version -> E403 'cannot publish over previously published version' -> infinite re-fail, registry frozen. Fix: derive the next version from `npm view <name> version` (highest actually published) + patch past it, AND move the git-auto-commit BEFORE npm publish so the repo version advances even on a publish hiccup. After fixing, every push bumps+publishes. A green CI run + `npm view <name> version` advancing is the witness. Also: a golden/regression test that multiplies by a runtime DISPLAY scale (e.g. reliefScale) tracks a consumer knob not the source-of-truth artifact -> it breaks on an intended default change with a clean Nx ratio across all samples; fix by sampling the raw artifact (scale=1) in the lock, not the scaled output.